  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe use an iterative optimization method that builds upon the previous work but introduces enhancements to better navigate the search space and find improved candidates. The core idea involves:\n\n- Generating diverse initial sequences through multi-modal initialization schemes (raised_cosine, gaussian, blackman, and random variations)\n- Employing multi-start gradient-based optimization using a specially designed objective function and constraints\n- Using a combination of local refinement via linear programs and global exploration to avoid getting stuck in local minima\n- Carefully scheduling evaluations and solver time to respect the overall timeout while continuing to refine the best candidate\n\nThe main bottleneck in previous approaches was the excessive dependency on local search polishing (which often didn't yield significant improvements) and potentially suboptimal initial sequences. Our new approach aims to:\n\n- Accelerate optimization through a mix of random and systematic initialization\n- Reduce the computational overhead by using a more efficient evaluation schedule\n- Maintain strict adherence to the objective function's behavior while using surrogate optimizations\n- Use a multi-start strategy that doesn't rely fully on the previous parent candidate\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nOur optimization minimizes the function:\n\n  `score = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a, mode='full')) / sum(a)^2`\n\nWhere:\n- a must be a non-negative sequence of floats\n- The convolution is performed over the full convolution length (2*n-1)\n\nWe implement a two-phase optimization:\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm proceeds as follows:\n\n1. Global setup:\n   - Set random seed (if provided)\n   - Define working copy of best candidate and save the worst case fallback\n   - If parent sequence (height_sequence_1) is available, evaluate it as starting point\n\n2. Initialization phase:\n   - Generate up to 20 candidate sequences using:\n     * Raised cosine + jitter and variations\n     * Gaussian with randomized sigma\n     * Blackman window variants\n     * Random bumps with randomized widths and positions\n   - Each candidate has normalized sum around 0.7 (intentionally not 1.0) to allow more significant perturbations\n\n3. Optimization phase:\n   - Initialize with the best performing candidate from the initialization phase\n   - For each candidate, perform:\n      * Full evaluation of the score (to get exact measurement)\n      * Build gradient information if possible (though non-gradient based for induced robustness)\n      * Use bounded conjugate gradient descent if gradient info is available\n      * Fallback to penalty-based optimization if gradient is problematic\n   - Maintain a best candidate history and use exponential backoff for trials\n   * After ~1500 seconds, aggressively sample only the best performing candidates\n\n4. Refinement phase:\n   - Once time is low (<<10 seconds), get an FFT-like optimization on the best candidate\n   - Use sophisticated smoothing only when necessary based on histogram analysis\n\n5. Return best candidate found, with fallback to the most promising during initialization if no significantly good candidate is found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\nAdvantageous initialization points include:\n   - Jittered raised cosine with varying parameters\n   - Randomized Gaussian distribution with normalized sum\n   - Perturbed Blackman window with constraints\n   - Random bump sequences with randomized centers and widths\n\nWe vary these parameters randomly within safe bounds (0.1 to 0.3) to ensure diversity, but good candidates are identified based on normalized performance metrics.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nTime is scheduled to allow ~5-10 iterations of gradient evaluation for longer sequences (like 1000-2000 elements) while maintaining ~75-85% idle DB cache availability.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nCandidates are validated explicitly:\n   - All elements non-negative\n   - Sum above 0.01\n   - Random perturbations kept small enough\n\nWe return the sequence with the best score found via objective function calls, falling back during initial setup.",
